; findv with its operators expanded (previous slide), as basic blocks for abstract interpretation.
; The call (pred v i) is written as the opaque test pred(v, i), declared in the slide's prims, so
; that the drawing stays small; it tells nothing about v or i.
; ##vector-length returns at most maxfix, so i < len keeps i + 1 a fixnum: the overflow check of
; fx+? always succeeds, the fixnum? test too, and the generic and flonum additions are never reached.
function findv(v)
A:  goto L(i=0)
L:  if vector?(v) goto M else goto K
K:  fail
M:  len = ##vector-length(v)
    if >=(i, len) goto N else goto P
N:  return #f
P:  if pred(v, i) goto R else goto S
R:  return i
S:  if fixnum?(i) goto T else goto U
T:  i2 = fx+?(i, 1)
    if i2 goto T2 else goto O
T2: goto L(i=i2)
O:  i3 = ##+(i, 1)
    goto L(i=i3)
U:  if flonum?(i) goto W else goto X
W:  i4 = fl+(i, 1.0)
    goto L(i=i4)
X:  i5 = ##+(i, 1)
    goto L(i=i5)
